WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:11.120
tudying languages is a privilege. When you analyze&nbsp;
language and everyday speech you start to realize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:14.960
that there is an astonishing amount of wonder&nbsp;
in this system that we take for granted.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:20.160
Linguists questioned the obvious, which&nbsp;
is language, and got answers that forever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:25.520
changed mankind’s understanding of Language&nbsp;
and human nature. In this article, you will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:30.880
see what linguists know that is not so evident&nbsp;
to other people. So let’s see what we’ve got.
&nbsp;

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:36.400
We all speak one language.
One of the main discoveries of modern linguistics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:41.440
is that it made us aware that all the languages&nbsp;
we speak are similar in astonishing respects;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:46.800
they manifest the same pattern, follow the same&nbsp;
rules, they are learnt in exactly the same way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:51.840
and that all the differences are only superficial.&nbsp;
So, in a sense we all speak the same language.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:56.240
This was captured by Chomsky in an excellent&nbsp;
metaphor in an excellent book of his titled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:00.800
Language and Mind in which he&nbsp;
says that if a Martian scientist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:05.040
somebody with a different kind of intelligence,&nbsp;
were to study the world’s languages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:10.320
he would conclude that they are all dialects&nbsp;
of a single language embodying a “universal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:16.400
grammar” reflecting a hardwired, genetically&nbsp;
determined linguistic module inherent in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:19.520
human brain. A great deal of theories complied&nbsp;
a huge base of evidence from topologically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:24.240
different languages to show that this is&nbsp;
indeed the case. I will mention, in passing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:29.280
one of probably the main arguments used to&nbsp;
prove this. Take a child that was born in China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:33.120
and have him raised in Saudi Arabia&nbsp;
and he’ll grow up speaking Arabic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:38.480
What does that say to you? It says that a&nbsp;
human child is hardwired to learn any language&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:43.920
and that all human languages, say Mandarin and&nbsp;
Arabic in this case are fundamentally the same,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:49.840
if they weren’t, the task of child to learn any&nbsp;
language would be impossible. This has massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:56.400
implications, the greatest of which is that all&nbsp;
the 7000 human languages have the same source&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:01.280
and that every language spoken today evolved&nbsp;
from the same great-great…great-grandmother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:04.480
tongue, the first language of homo sapiens.&nbsp;
Another implication is that if a Martian would fly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:10.000
past our planet, he would hear Japanese and Ojibwe&nbsp;
like we hear British and American English. 
&nbsp;

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:24.320
anguage change is natural:
People so soften combat language&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:29.520
change and are feared by it. Linguists know that&nbsp;
natural language change is not bad for a language.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:35.120
Adopting loan words from other languages,&nbsp;
losing inflection, cases, genders, articles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:40.640
and formerly distinct sounds…This happens in&nbsp;
all languages and is nothing to worry about.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:45.680
For a linguist, it’s an interesting phenomenon,&nbsp;
worth analyzing. For some people it’s proof for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:50.240
the demise of language, the end of civilized&nbsp;
culture and the beginning of the dying of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:54.880
proper language. But languages constantly change&nbsp;
and this is certainly not how languages die.
&nbsp;

00:02:55.440 --> 00:03:00.160
Too many people fear language change. Many people&nbsp;
think that if we don’t attempt to regulate the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:04.800
“correct” way of speaking — from spelling to&nbsp;
pronunciation to word choice — then we will all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:11.280
plunge into anarchy or some other misfortune.&nbsp;
It’s a sham. Language constantly evolves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:16.960
and even those political/cultural&nbsp;
elites who claim to use it “correctly”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:18.347
fail to do so themselves; this is why you find&nbsp;
split infinitives in written opinions of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.347 --> 00:03:21.520
U.S. Supreme Court, for example. (And even those&nbsp;
who fancy themselves guardians of The Correct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:28.480
Way of Speaking can’t even agree on whether split&nbsp;
infinitives are kosher). Societies rise and fall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:33.600
but not because too many people started to say&nbsp;
“ain’t” when they should have said “isn’t.”
&nbsp;

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:39.040
There are no bad grammars:
One of the things you know when you start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:43.520
studying languages is that you realize how stupid&nbsp;
is the claim that says there are bad grammars.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:47.760
Linguists know that all dialects,&nbsp;
even those considered “bad grammar”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:51.920
by many people, are as fully formed&nbsp;
and rule-bound as any other language.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:57.600
Someone who uses Appalachian dialect&nbsp;
or African American Vernacular English&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:04.080
(to use two U.S. examples), is not simply ignorant&nbsp;
of grammatical rules taught in school, or speaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:08.880
“sloppily.” They are following a different set&nbsp;
of rules, which are consistent within their own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:13.920
dialect. Sometimes the linguistic complexity&nbsp;
of languages associated with underprivileged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:20.400
societies and that we call bad grammars far&nbsp;
exceeds that of modern and privileged societies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:27.760
William Labov in fact analyzed African American&nbsp;
Vernacular English and showed us that it is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:33.280
beautiful language with a sophisticated structure&nbsp;
and has many features that Standard English lacks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:40.240
Read more about this is Labov’s own book titled&nbsp;
Language in the inner city: Studies in the Black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:44.960
English Vernacular. You can get it [here].
There are monolingual linguists:
&nbsp;

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:50.987
I don’t know how to stress this further. Being a&nbsp;
linguist does not equal being a polyglot. While it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.987 --> 00:04:56.320
is true that linguists study all languages, this&nbsp;
does not mean that they engage in learning them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.320 --> 00:05:01.280
They only study them to test certain hypotheses&nbsp;
related to how language works. Linguists know that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:06.480
there are monolingual linguists, there are even&nbsp;
linguists who advise against learning languages.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:12.160
Seriously, people should stop using “linguist”&nbsp;
and “polyglot” or “language lover” synonymously.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:16.400
The surest way to annoy a linguist is to&nbsp;
ask: “so how many languages do you speak?”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:20.080
Read about what linguists do [here].
Language is fractal:
&nbsp;

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:24.160
Linguists know that you really can’t&nbsp;
fully describe any human language.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:29.440
It seems to be impossible to produce enough rules&nbsp;
to fully describe any natural language. You always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:34.080
find that there are valid expressions that your&nbsp;
rules forbid and invalid expressions that your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:39.280
rules permit. (That’s what we mean when we&nbsp;
say “all grammars leak.”) Of course you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:45.280
create new rules to cover those exceptions, but&nbsp;
teams have spent decades at this without closing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:50.640
all the holes–despite creating tens of thousands&nbsp;
of rules. This is one reason linguists have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:55.520
trouble taking grammar “prescriptivists”&nbsp;
seriously; we know that no description of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.520 --> 00:06:00.160
grammar small enough to fit in a single volume&nbsp;
can be anything more than a set of guidelines.
&nbsp;

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:07.840
Linguists know that everybody who speaks one&nbsp;
language, can learn others, if they want to.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:14.800
The mathematics behind linguistics (e.g. Automata&nbsp;
Theory and Chomsky’s hierarchy of languages),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:19.680
indicate that an astonishing amount of&nbsp;
intelligence is required to become a monoglot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:23.280
The more we study computational&nbsp;
linguistics and automata theory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:26.640
the more we understand this.
 
The simplest theoretical machine that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:32.160
could accurately parse a human language and&nbsp;
map to meanings, would be incredibly complex.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:36.880
Surprisingly, the amount of complexity one&nbsp;
must add in order to parse a second language&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:41.920
is almost negligible. In other words, there is&nbsp;
no real difference in the intelligence necessary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:47.120
to become a monoglot or a polyglot.
 
Perceived differences in difficulty have more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:52.880
to do with learning as an adult vs. as a child&nbsp;
(during the critical period), and with enjoyment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:57.680
of learning and willingness to work.
 
I’m sure linguists know so much more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:02.080
can be said in a blog post. Linguists&nbsp;
changed our perceptions about language,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:08.000
changed the way we regard minority languages,&nbsp;
and most importantly linguists made us aware that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:14.000
no language is inferior. Join the bandwagon to&nbsp;
recognize the efforts of linguists by subscribing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:22.701
by email and sharing the video. Thank you very&nbsp;
much for reading. I will see you in my next video.

